# ###########################################################################
# LINE ENDINGS ARE THE REPOSITORY'S DECISION, NOT THE CHECKOUT'S.
#
# `git add` on a Windows machine with core.autocrlf=true warns that "LF will be
# replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" for every text file in the
# tree. For most of them that is cosmetic. For one of them it is a break:
# database/bootstrap/init/00_bootstrap.sh is executed INSIDE the Postgres container,
# and a shebang followed by \r fails with `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M` -- which
# would only appear when somebody sets up a fresh environment, i.e. at the
# worst possible moment and to the person least able to diagnose it.
#
# So the working copy is normalised to LF regardless of platform. Nothing here
# needs CRLF: there are no .bat or .cmd files, and PowerShell, Node and Postgres
# all read LF happily on Windows.
# ###########################################################################
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the file where it is load-bearing rather than tidy.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf

# Never touched, never diffed. Left here so a stray archive cannot be
# line-ending-mangled on the way in if one is ever committed deliberately.
*.tar.gz binary
*.tgz    binary
*.zip    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.pdf    binary
